the minimum. For a specific case, ask HR, a labor attorney or the Oregon Bureau of Labor and Industries (BOLI).", "title": "Oregon (OR) minimum wage and overtime", "topic": "Employment law: Oregon", "source": "U.S. DOL state minimum wage table (updated Nov 5, 2026); BOLI bulletin Apr 27, 2026; checked 2026-11-05", "confidence": "high"}], [648, "field", {"id": "emp-state-wa-3", "url": "https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/minimum-wage/state", "text": "Minimum wage in Washington (as of Nov 5, 2026): $17.13 per hour in 2026; $17.73 on January 1, 2027 (announced September 30, 2026). Higher local rates include Seattle ($21.30 in 2026), SeaTac, Tukwila, Renton, Bellingham, Everett, Burien and unincorporated King County. Exempt salary 2026: $80,168.40 a year; 2027: $92,196 (51+ employees) or $82,976.40 (50 or fewer). The federal minimum is $7.25; the highest of federal, state and local rates that applies where the work is done must be paid. Overtime in Washington: 1.5x over 40 hours a week; long workdays alone do not trigger overtime. Prevailing wage and union jobs usually pay well above the minimum. For a specific case, ask HR, a labor attorney or the Washington State Department of Labor and Industries (L&I).", "title": "Washington (WA) minimum wage and overtime", "topic": "Employment law: Washington", "source": "U.S. DOL state minimum wage table (updated Nov 5, 2026); L&I news release 26-17 (Sep 30, 2026); checked 2026-11-05", "confidence": "high"}], [1926, "field", {"id": "separation-cover-marking-1", "url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/virginia/12VAC5-590-1150", "text": "The common rule (from the Ten States Standards and state rules such as Virginia's) is that water mains should be at least 10 ft horizontally from sanitary sewers and sewer manholes, measured edge to edge. Under Virginia's rule, where local conditions prevent 10 ft, the water main may be closer if its bottom is at least 18 inches above the top of the sewer; if that can't be done, the sewer must be built of water-main-grade pipe and pressure tested in place, or the department may approve concrete encasement or another barrier. Water pipes must not pass through or touch any part of a sewer manhole. States vary on the details and on rules for storm sewers and force mains, so check your state rules and project specs.", "title": "Water and sewer separation: horizontal 10 ft rule", "topic": "Installation: separation, cover, tracer wire, warning tape", "source": "Virginia 12VAC5-590-1150, Separation of water mains and sanitary sewers (follows Ten States Standards approach)", "confidence": "high"}], [1927, "field", {"id": "separation-cover-marking-2", "url": "https://www.law.cornell.edu/regulations/virginia/12VAC5-590-1150", "text": "At crossings, the common rule is at least 18 inches of vertical separation between the bottom of the water main and the top of the sewer, with the water main above the sewer. Per Virginia's rule (Ten States approach): where that can't be met, sewers crossing over or under water mains must be built of water-main-grade pipe for 10 ft each side of the crossing. If the water main must go under the sewer, you need at least 18 inches between the bottom of the sewer and top of the water main, structural support for the sewer so it can't settle onto the main, and a full length of water pipe centered on the crossing so its joints are as far from the sewer as possible. Practical tip: when laying water main across a sewer, center a full stick on the crossing. Check your state and utility rules, which can differ.", "title": "Water and sewer separation: crossings and the 18 inch rule", "topic": "Installation: separation, cover, tracer wire, warning tape", "source": "Virginia 12VAC5-590-1150, Separation of water mains and sanitary sewers (follows Ten States Standards approach)", "confidence": "high"}], [2255, "field", {"id": "stake-read-30", "text": "Survey stake and flagging colors vary by company and job, so no single color chart is universal; ask the surveyor's legend. Common practice: pink, orange or fluorescent flagging on survey stakes; blue tops for finished grade hubs; red or other colors for other grades. Utility locate marks follow the APWA uniform color code: white proposed excavation, pink temporary survey marks, red electric, yellow gas, oil or steam, orange communications and CATV, blue potable water, purple reclaimed water and irrigation, green sewer and storm drain. Survey paint is not a utility locate.", "title": "Color of stakes, flagging and paint", "topic": "Survey stakes: reading every kind of stake", "source": "Standard construction staking practice (US civil, utility and grading work); worked examples written for The Pipelayer's Bible"}], [2312, "field", {"id": "sym-4", "text": "The offset distance is usually written at the top of the lath, above the description: \"10 / SD\", \"5 / WALL / WF\". Many surveyors draw a circle around the offset number (a circled 5 = 5 ft off whatever the stake names); others write O/S or OS, or underline the offset twice, or put it above a line. L/O means line only: the stake is good for line, not for distance. A number in front of a slash on a utility lath is an offset distance, never a pipe size.", "title": "Offset marks: circled or underlined numbers", "topic": "Survey and plan symbols: stake marks, colors and plan symbols", "source": "Standard construction staking and plan drafting practice (US civil and utility work); symbols vary by agency and surveyor"}], [2821, "field", {"id": "dig-or-overview-1", "url": "https://digsafelyoregon.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/2026-Addendum-to-the-Oregon-Standards-Manual_Final.pdf", "kind": "summary", "text": "Oregon's dig rules are ORS 757.542 to 757.562 and OAR chapter 952, written by the Oregon Utility Notification Center (Oregon 811). Pre-mark the dig area in white, then call at least 2 full business days and not more than 10 business days before digging. Do not start until you have \"received a response from each operator of underground facilities in the area.\" Ticket life: the Center's 2026 Addendum says the rule was changed \"from 45 days to 30 days,\" effective January 8, 2026. Older copies of the rule and the Center's own FAQ page still say 45 calendar days, so the current number is not confirmed. Until you check with Oregon 811, plan on 30 days. Past ticket life, call in a new locate request. Tolerance zone: 24 inches around the outside of the line on all sides. Use hand tools or other non-invasive methods there. Hit a line: tell the operator right away; gas or hazardous liquid release, call 911. Don't bury it without the operator's OK.", "tier": "core", "state": "OR", "title": "Oregon 811 rules in short: wait before you dig, how long a ticket is good, hand-dig zone (Oregon 811)", "topic": "811 dig rules by state: wait before digging, ticket life, tolerance zone", "source": "OAR 952-001-0010, -0050, -0070, -0090 (Oregon Utility Notification Center); 2026 Addendum to the Oregon Standards Manual (Mar 12, 2026)", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "2026 Addendum, last updated March 12, 2026", "license": "gov-state", "effective": "2026-01-08", "confidence": "medium", "supersedes": "dig-or", "unconfirmed": "ticket life 30 vs 45 days: the official rule text with the 2026 change was not opened"}], [2857, "field", {"id": "dig-or-tickets-1", "url": "https://digsafelyoregon.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/2026-Addendum-to-the-Oregon-Standards-Manual_Final.pdf", "kind": "summary", "text": "Oregon ticket life: the 2026 Addendum to the Oregon Standards Manual (Oregon Utility Notification Center, March 12, 2026) lists a change to OAR 952-001-0010(23) that shortens ticket life \"from 45 days to 30 days,\" effective January 8, 2026. Copies of the rule dated through mid-2025 read \"the 45 calendar day period after an excavator provides notice,\" and the Center's FAQ page still said 45 on Nov 20, 2026. The official current rule text was not confirmed in this pass, so treat 30 days as the safe number and ask Oregon 811. There is no renewal. The rule says not to keep digging after ticket life ends \"unless you re-notify the Center\" (0090): that is a new locate request with the normal 2 full business days' notice. Do not start more than 10 business days after the notice without re-notifying. Keep the marks for the life of the job or of the ticket, whichever is shorter. Marks gone: stop and ask the Center for a remark. There is no legal \"priority\" locate. Less than 2 business days' notice is only for emergencies (0060).", "tier": "core", "state": "OR", "title": "Oregon 811: ticket life changed to 30 days in 2026 (was 45), no renewals", "topic": "811 tickets: types, ticket life, renewals and updates", "source": "OAR 952-001-0010, -0050, -0070, -0090 (Oregon Utility Notification Center); 2026 Addendum to the Oregon Standards Manual (Mar 12, 2026)", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "2026 Addendum, last updated March 12, 2026", "license": "gov-state", "effective": "2026-01-08", "confidence": "medium", "unconfirmed": "ticket life 30 vs 45 days: official current rule text not opened"}], [2900, "field", {"id": "dig-sc-tickets-1", "url": "https://sc811.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Excavator-Manual-2026.pdf", "kind": "summary", "text": "South Carolina ticket types (SC811 Excavator Manual 2026): Normal; Design (operators have 15 full working days; no digging on it); Emergency; Large Project; Subaqueous (underwater: 10 working days' notice, tolerance zone 15 feet either side). Large Project: a job expected to take more than 90 days, or that meets the highway, development or utility size tests. Notify the center at least 30 days before starting; a planning meeting is held within 15 days; each phase gets its own start and end dates and needs no updates while work stays inside them. Emergency: \"any sudden or unforeseen event that requires immediate action.\" Notify SC811 and the operators as soon as practicable; operators make contact within three hours. Calling it an emergency does not remove liability for damage. Bad weather: ice or snow can be an extraordinary circumstance that lets an operator post Code 110 with a later marking date. Complaints: file with the Attorney General's Office within 45 days of the incident. Fine amounts are not in the manual and were not confirmed.", "tier": "core", "state": "SC", "title": "South Carolina 811: ticket types, large projects, emergencies, complaints", "topic": "811 tickets: types, ticket life, renewals and updates", "source": "SC811 Excavator Manual 2026 pp. 9-23, 27-35; S.C. Code 58-36-10 to 58-36-120", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "2026 Excavator Manual", "license": "summary", "confidence": "medium", "unconfirmed": "fine amounts"}], [2979, "field", {"id": "sp-ca-trench-1", "url": "https://dir.ca.gov/Title8/1541_1.html", "kind": "summary", "text": "California (Cal/OSHA) requires a protective system for each employee in an excavation unless it is made entirely in stable rock, or it is less than 5 feet deep and a competent person's examination of the ground finds no sign of a potential cave-in (8 CCR 1541.1(a)(1)). That is the same 5 ft test as federal OSHA. California's sloping table (8 CCR 1541.1 Appendix B) has the same slopes as the federal one. What California adds is a permit: 8 CCR 1539 sends excavation and trench permits to 8 CCR 341, and the app's state table (checked 2026-11-19) carries it as a Cal/OSHA permit for trenches or excavations 5 ft or deeper that a person enters, with emergency repair of underground facilities exempt. Section 341 itself was not re-read on 2026-11-20, so the permit details are not confirmed here beyond that. The trench shield rule matches the federal one: dig no more than 2 feet below the bottom of the support (8 CCR 1541.1(e)(2)(A)).", "tier": "core", "state": "CA", "title": "California trench protection depth and the Cal/OSHA excavation permit", "topic": "State safety plan: trench and excavation", "source": "8 CCR 1541.1(a)(1), (e)(2)(A); 8 CCR 1539; 8 CCR 341", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "Title 8 CCR, section text read 2026-11-20 (Register 2011, No. 50)", "license": "gov-state", "confidence": "medium", "unconfirmed": "yes"}]]$kb$::jsonb) e where k.version = 'b3e91f6c47d2' and k.ord = (e->>0)::int;
